diagnosis_text:
OSPF across the core is healthy and stable; there is no flapping router or backbone link to restart. Evidence:
- All core adjacencies are Full and steady. On router_core_1, router_core_2, and router_core_3, “show ip ospf neighbor detail” shows Full/DR or Full/Backup with “Most recent state change ~53–99s ago” and “6 state changes.” The “6 state changes” correspond to the normal OSPF state-machine progression (Down→Init→2-Way→ExStart→Exchange→Loading→Full) at startup, not repeated flaps.
- FRR logs on all three cores contain only initial configuration reads and one-time DD negotiation, with no repeated neighbor down/up messages.
- OSPF interfaces have stable Hello/Dead timers, fixed DR/BDR roles, and 1 adjacent neighbor each; no MTU mismatches.
- Routing tables show consistent OSPF routes: 10.200.0.0/24 (server farm) is present on router_core_1 and router_core_2 via router_core_3, with normal ages and no oscillation.
- Data-plane looks clean: reachability tests show 0% loss; ethtool reports links up; ip -s link and tc stats show zero errors/drops across eth0/eth1/eth2 on all cores.

Conclusion: The NOC likely interpreted the initial convergence counters (“6 state changes”) as flaps right after a recent reboot. There is no current instability and no device or link requires a restart. Corrective action: none required. For clarity and to reduce DR/BDR election noise on /31 core links, consider setting OSPF network type to point-to-point; also enable more verbose OSPF neighbor-change logging/alerting to distinguish initial convergence from true flapping. 
